  "tjdHamzatWasl": "A connecting hamzah at the start of a word. It is pronounced only when you begin with that word; when joining from the previous word it is silent, so read straight through it.",
  "tjdSilent": "A letter written in the mushaf but not pronounced, such as the alif after the plural waw. Skip it in recitation.",
  "tjdLaamShamsiyyah": "The lam of the definite article before a sun letter is not pronounced: double the sun letter instead, as in ٱلشَّمْس → ash-shams.",
  "tjdMaddNormal": "A natural lengthening: hold the long vowel for two counts, no more and no less.",
  "tjdMaddPermissible": "A lengthening where a madd letter meets a hamzah in the next word, or a stop creates a sukun. Hold it two, four or six counts, but stay consistent through the recitation.",
  "tjdMaddNecessary": "A lengthening required by an original sukun or a shaddah after the madd letter, including the opening letters of surahs. Hold it six counts.",
  "tjdQalqalah": "An echoing bounce on the letters ق ط ب ج د when they carry a sukun. Release the letter with a slight rebound, stronger when stopping on it.",
  "tjdMaddObligatory": "A madd letter followed by a hamzah in the same word. Hold it four to five counts.",
  "tjdIkhfaShafawi": "A meem with sukun before ب: close the lips lightly and hide the meem with a nasal sound of two counts.",
  "tjdIkhfa": "A nun with sukun or tanwin before one of the fifteen ikhfa letters: hide the nun with a nasal sound of two counts, with the tongue ready for the next letter.",
  "tjdIdghamShafawi": "A meem with sukun before another meem: merge them into one doubled meem with a nasal sound of two counts.",
  "tjdIqlab": "A nun with sukun or tanwin before ب changes into a meem, hidden with a nasal sound of two counts and lightly closed lips.",
  "tjdIdghamGhunnah": "A nun with sukun or tanwin before ي ن م و at the start of the next word merges into that letter, with a nasal sound of two counts.",
  "tjdIdghamNoGhunnah": "A nun with sukun or tanwin before ل or ر merges completely into that letter, with no nasal sound.",
  "tjdIdghamMutajanisayn": "Two letters from the same point of articulation, the first with sukun, merge into one doubled letter, as in قَد تَّبَيَّنَ.",
  "tjdIdghamMutaqaribayn": "Two letters from close points of articulation, the first with sukun, merge into the second, as in قُل رَّبِّ.",
  "tjdGhunnah": "A nasal resonance held for two counts on a meem or nun with shaddah.",
